: Cross-check listing: seed-b (bootstrap/roots/b) rendered in format C, so that
: seed-c can assemble it. The bytes are the same as seed-b.oct; the comments
: below are seed-b.oct's, verbatim, and describe seed-b and its format B.

: seed-b: the first loader of bootstrap root B. Written by hand, byte by byte,
: separately from seed-a, in its own format.
:
: What it does: reads a listing from standard input and writes the bytes it
: describes to standard output.
:
: Format B, the format of this file:
:   - a byte is a group of exactly three octal digits, 000 to 377: digits
:     0-7, and the first digit of a group only 0-3;
:   - the three digits of a group are contiguous: nothing may stand between
:     them;
:   - between groups: space (040), tab (011), newline (012), or ';', which
:     starts a comment that runs to the end of the line.
: Anything else is refused: the digits 8 and 9, any letter, carriage return
: (015), a separator or ';' inside a group, a first digit above 3, and an
: incomplete group at the end of input.
:
: Exit status: 0 when the whole input was valid and every byte was written;
: 1 on a refused input, a failed read or a short write. Output is written as
: it is decoded, so on exit 1 the bytes already written must be discarded.
:
: Self-reproduction: `seed-b < seed-b.oct` writes exactly the bytes of `seed-b`.
: Cross-check: `seed-b < ../a/seed-a.oct` writes exactly the bytes of seed-a.
:
: Layout: one ELF64 header (000-077), one program header (100-167), code
: (170-432). The file is loaded at virtual address 0x400000. Every offset in
: this file is octal; a jump's target is the offset of the next instruction
: plus or minus its displacement, also in octal. Total 283 bytes (0433).
:
: How it differs from seed-a, on purpose: base 8, not 16; fixed three-digit
: groups, not free pairs; separators only at group boundaries; ';' comments,
: not '#'; the accumulator starts at 0 with a separate digit counter (r13)
: instead of a sentinel bit; the fail exit sits in the middle of the code,
: and the loop is closed by a near jump.
:
: Machine state at entry: only rsp is used as given by the kernel. r12, r13
: and r14 are set first; eax, edi, esi, edx are written before they are read.
:   r12  accumulator of the current group
:   r13  digits read in the current group (0-2; 3 means "write it")
:   r14  1 inside a comment, 0 outside
:   [rsp] one-byte buffer for read and for write

